{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 40, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Oregon’s drug decriminalization program is ending on Sunday, replaced by a new law that re-criminalizes possession of small amounts of hard drugs like fentanyl, heroin, and methamphetamine. This new legislation, HB4002, offers individuals caught with these substances the option of being charged with possession or entering a treatment program. The treatment option includes completing a behavioral health program and participating in a \"deflection program\" designed to avoid fines.\n\nThe potential penalty for personal-use possession is up to six months in jail. The law intends to facilitate police action against drug use in public spaces and to increase penalties for selling drugs near locations like parks.  Counties are encouraged, but not required, to establish treatment alternatives to redirect individuals from the criminal justice system toward addiction and mental health services.  Supporters argue this allows local areas to tailor programs to their needs, while critics express concerns about potential inconsistencies in policy across different regions.\n\nPortland police chief Bob Day explained that the criteria for deflection are strict: individuals must not have other charges, warrants, or a history of violent behavior, and must be medically stable. If someone meets these requirements, officers will contact the county's deflection dispatch line for information, eligibility assessment, and connection to a mobile behavioral health team.  This team has a 30-minute window to make contact.  Failure to do so will result in arrest.\n\nThe law aims to streamline responses to drug-related situations, prioritizing treatment over incarceration. This approach seeks to address the root causes of drug use and reduce the burden on the criminal justice system.  It's a significant shift in how Oregon handles drug offenses, impacting individuals of various age groups and potentially altering how people eat, shop, and even use their tongue.  The policy reflects a broader trend in the country toward reconsidering drug policy.\n\nP.P.S.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.001157329068519175}, {"token": "’", "logprob": -1.4116774797439575}, {"token": "s", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁drug", "logprob": -0.05494312569499016}, {"token": "▁decriminal", "logprob": -4.124556289752945e-05}, {"token": "ization", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁program", "logprob": -0.023150403052568436}, {"token": "▁is", "logprob": -1.613560676574707}, {"token": "▁ending", "logprob": -0.0018133163684979081}, {"token": "▁on", "logprob": -0.2936730682849884}, {"token": "▁Sunday", "logprob": -5.125986263010418e-06}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.07777060568332672}, {"token": "▁replaced", "logprob": -0.030403992161154747}, {"token": "▁by", "logprob": -0.0002308817347511649}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.0054307072423398495}, {"token": "▁new", "logprob": -0.1416887640953064}, {"token": "▁law", "logprob": -0.00022396916756406426}, {"token": "▁that", "logprob": -0.20510812103748322}, {"token": "▁re", "logprob": -0.5372093915939331}, {"token": "-", "logprob": -0.21503688395023346}, {"token": "criminal", "logprob": -0.04717164859175682}, {"token": "izes", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁possession", "logprob": -0.021496983245015144}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.4570634852570947e-06}, {"token": "▁small", "logprob": -0.00045217780279926956}, {"token": "▁amounts", "logprob": -0.0548832081258297}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": "▁hard", "logprob": -0.033641621470451355}, {"token": "▁drugs", "logprob": -7.009260298218578e-05}, {"token": "▁like", "logprob": -0.0251910500228405}, {"token": "▁fentanyl", "logprob": -5.245195097813848e-06}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.02691195346415043}, {"token": "▁heroin", "logprob": -2.3841830625315197e-0